|~ `::map` and what is declared under it: its constructor and the
   refusals of a payload it cannot read, and the words of maps. A map's
   elements are its values, and its keys are the shape that travels with
   them: a word whose answer names the map keeps the keys of the entries
   it keeps and answers under the tags of the map, and `map`, whose
   answer is any value, answers under none. ~|

:map
  |~~ The kind of a map, the value of a `{…}` literal: named facts under
      keywords, the shape a JSON object arrives in. A map literal is also
      the shape an answer takes, each value a group run on the subject,
      `{:id /id :n (/tags | count)}`; `/k` reads a field, and `map` over
      a map runs a step on each value under its key. A JSON object is a
      map literal too, its string keys read as keywords, so JSON pastes
      into a query as it is; one literal is written in one of the two
      forms, and a key written twice holds its last value. A map comes
      apart into its pairs `[key value]` by `entries` and back by
      `tag ::map`, which reads a key written as a string or a number as
      the keyword of its text.
      ::snippet~(::map | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals. ::link~(::proj | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:map/isAMap
  |~~ Braces make a map. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | type | eq ::map)

:map/buildsAMap
  |~~ The constructor of a map answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::map{:a 1} | eq {:a 1})

:map/readsJson
  |~~ A JSON object is a map literal. ~~|
  ::law~("{\"a\": 1, \"b\": [true, null]}" | parse | apply / | eq {:a 1 :b [true null]})

:map/keepsTheLastOfAKey
  |~~ A key written twice keeps the last value. ~~|
  ::law~("{:a 1 :a 2}" | parse | apply / | eq {:a 2})

:map/buildsFromPairs
  |~~ A vector of pairs under `::map` is a map. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1] ["b" 2]] | tag ::map | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/buildsBackFromEntries
  |~~ The entries of a map build it back. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | entries | tag ::map | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/construct
  |~~ Reads a map as the bare map beneath its tags, and a vector or a set
      of pairs `[key value]` as the map of those entries in their order, a
      key written twice holding its last value.
      ::link~(::map | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/PayloadNotMapError
  |~~ `::map` reads a map as the map it is and a vector or a set as its
      pairs; a payload of another kind is refused.
  ~~|

:map/ElementNotPairError
  |~~ Each element of the vector `::map` builds from is a pair `[key
      value]`; `:index` names the first that is not.
  ~~|

:map/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key of a pair is a keyword, or a string or a number naming the
      keyword of its text; `:index` names the pair whose key is another
      value.
  ~~|

:map/construct/readsTheMapBeneathItsTags
  |~~ A map under a tag reads as the bare map. ~~|
  ::law~(::map(::Box{:a 1}) | eq {:a 1})

:map/construct/readsASetOfPairs
  |~~ A set of pairs builds a map as a vector of them does. ~~|
  ::law~(#[[:a 1]] | tag ::map | eq {:a 1})

:map/construct/keepsTheLastValueOfAKeyWrittenTwice
  |~~ A key written twice among the pairs holds its last value. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1] [:a 2]] | tag ::map | eq {:a 2})

:map/construct/namesAKeyByTheTextOfANumber
  |~~ A number names the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 "x"]] | tag ::map | eq {:"1" "x"})

:map/PayloadNotMapError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::map(1) !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/PayloadNotMapError ::number])

:map/ElementNotPairError/refusesAPairOfOne
  |~~ An element of one place is no pair, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1] [:b]] | tag ::map !| [type /index] | eq [::map/ElementNotPairError 1])

:map/ElementNotPairError/refusesAPairOfThree
  |~~ An element of three places is no pair either. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1 2]] | tag ::map !| [type /index] | eq [::map/ElementNotPairError 0])

:map/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesAVectorKey
  |~~ A vector key is refused at its pair. ~~|
  ::law~([[[1] 2]] | tag ::map !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::map/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0])

:map/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesABooleanKey
  |~~ A boolean names no key. ~~|
  ::law~([[true 1]] | tag ::map !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/KeyNotKeywordError ::boolean])

:map/count
  |~~ Answers how many entries the map holds, `{:a 1 :b 2} | count`
      answering `2` and the empty map `0`, a map under a tag counting the
      entries beneath it. `count` takes no value: the entries to count
      are chosen before it, `filter ~(gt 1) | count`. A vector, a set and
      a string answer by a `count` of their own, a string its characters.
      ::link~(::map/empty | doc) ::link~(::vec/count | doc)
      ::link~(::string/count | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/count/answer
  |~~ How many entries the map holds. ~~|
  ::number

:map/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `count` takes no value; the entries to count are chosen before it,
      `filter ~(gt 1) | count`.
  ~~|

:map/count/countsTheEntries
  |~~ Three entries count three. ~~|
  ::law~({:x 1 :y 2 :z 3} | count | eq 3)

:map/count/countsNothingInTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map counts no entry. ~~|
  ::law~({} | count | eq 0)

:map/count/countsBeneathATag
  |~~ A map under a tag counts the entries beneath it. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 1 :b 2} | count | eq 2)

:map/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAPredicate
  |~~ A predicate given to `count` is refused; `filter` chooses first. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | count ~(gt 1) !| type | eq ::map/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:map/empty
  |~~ Answers whether the map holds no entry, `{} | empty` answering
      `true`; it takes no value. A key held with a null value is an entry,
      so `{:a null} | empty` answers `false`. A vector and a set answer by
      an `empty` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/count | doc) ::link~(::vec/empty | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/empty/answer
  |~~ `true` when the map holds no entry, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/empty/holdsOfTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map is empty. ~~|
  ::law~({} | empty)

:map/empty/failsOfAKeyWithANullValue
  |~~ A key held with a null value is an entry. ~~|
  ::law~({:a null} | empty | not)

:map/first
  |~~ Answers the value of the first entry, in the order the map holds its
      entries, the order they were written, or null for the empty map; it
      takes no value. The entry itself, key and value, is `entries |
      first`, while `keys | first` answers the key first by name. The
      first entries of a map are `take 2`. A vector and a set answer their
      first element by a `first` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/last | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/first | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/first/answer
  |~~ The value of the first entry, null for the empty map. ~~|
  ::any

:map/first/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `first` answers one value; the first entries of a map are `take 2`. ~~|

:map/first/answersTheFirstValue
  |~~ The value of the first entry answers. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | first | eq 1)

:map/first/followsTheOrderWritten
  |~~ The first entry is the first written, whatever its key. ~~|
  ::law~({:b 1 :a 2} | first | eq 1)

:map/first/answersNullForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map answers null. ~~|
  ::law~({} | first | eq null)

:map/first/readsTheEntryByEntries
  |~~ The first entry, key and value, is read by `entries`. ~~|
  ::law~({:b 1 :a 2} | entries | first | eq [:b 1])

:map/first/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesACount
  |~~ A count given to `first` is refused; `take` takes it. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | first 2 !| type | eq ::map/first/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:map/last
  |~~ Answers the value of the last entry, in the order the map holds its
      entries, or null for the empty map; it takes no value. The entry
      itself, key and value, is `entries | last`. A vector and a set
      answer their last element by a `last` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/first | doc) ::link~(::vec/last | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/last/answer
  |~~ The value of the last entry, null for the empty map. ~~|
  ::any

:map/last/answersTheLastValue
  |~~ The value of the last entry answers. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | last | eq 2)

:map/last/answersNullForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map answers null. ~~|
  ::law~({} | last | eq null)

:map/sum
  |~~ Answers the sum of the values, each a number, `{:a 10 :b 20} | sum`
      answering `30` and the empty map `0`; it takes no value. A value
      that is no number is refused with its key, and a field of each value
      is read first by `map`, `map ~/amount | sum`. A vector and a set sum
      their elements by a `sum` of their own, and two fields of one record
      add by `sumOf /x /y`.
      ::link~(::map/avg | doc) ::link~(::vec/sum | doc)
      ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/sum/answer
  |~~ The sum of the values, `0` for the empty map. ~~|
  ::number

:map/sum/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `sum` adds the numbers the map holds; `:key` names the first entry
      whose value is none, and a field is read first, `map ~/amount | sum`.
  ~~|

:map/sum/ElementNotNumberError/key
  |~~ The key of the first entry whose value is no number. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/sum/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `sum` takes no value: a field of each value is read first by
      `map`, `map ~/amount | sum`.
  ~~|

:map/sum/addsTheValues
  |~~ The sum adds every value. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 10 :b 20} | sum | eq 30)

:map/sum/answersZeroForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map sums to zero. ~~|
  ::law~({} | sum | eq 0)

:map/sum/sumsAFieldReadFirst
  |~~ A field of each value is read by `map` before the sum. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:amount 1} :b {:amount 2}} | map ~/amount | sum | eq 3)

:map/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the values is refused under its key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b "two"} | sum
    !| [type /actualType /key]
    |  eq [::map/sum/ElementNotNumberError ::string :b])

:map/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesANull
  |~~ A null value is no number either. ~~|
  ::law~({:a null} | sum !| [type /key] | eq [::map/sum/ElementNotNumberError :a])

:map/sum/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAField
  |~~ A field given to `sum` is refused; `map` reads it first. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | sum /a !| type | eq ::map/sum/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:map/avg
  |~~ Answers the mean of the values, each a number, `{:a 1 :b 2} | avg`
      answering `1.5`, or null for the empty map, which has no mean, the
      mean of numbers staying among the finite doubles,
      `{:a 1e308 :b -1e308} | avg` answering `0`; it takes no value. A value that is no number is refused with its key,
      and a field of each value is read first by `map`, `map ~/amount |
      avg`. A vector and a set answer by an `avg` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/sum | doc) ::link~(::vec/avg | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/avg/answer
  |~~ The mean of the values, null for the empty map. ~~|
  #[::number ::null]

:map/avg/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `avg` takes the mean of the numbers the map holds; `:key` names the
      first entry whose value is none.
  ~~|

:map/avg/ElementNotNumberError/key
  |~~ The key of the first entry whose value is no number. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/avg/answersTheMean
  |~~ The mean of one and three is two. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 3} | avg | eq 2)

:map/avg/answersNullForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map has no mean. ~~|
  ::law~({} | avg | eq null)

:map/avg/averagesTheOppositeEnds
  |~~ The mean of a number near one end of the doubles and its negation is zero. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1e308 :b -1e308} | avg | eq 0)

:map/avg/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the values is refused under its key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b "two"} | avg
    !| [type /actualType /key]
    |  eq [::map/avg/ElementNotNumberError ::string :b])

:map/min
  |~~ Answers the least value in the one order of values, a number before
      a string, or null for the empty map: `{:a 3 :b 1} | min` answers
      `1`. A key the call gives, code run on each value, ranks the values,
      `{:a {:n 2} :b {:n 1}} | min ~(/n)` answering `{:n 1}`,
      and a key under `desc` ranks in reverse. The entry of the least
      value, key and value, is `entries | min ~(/1)`. A vector and a set
      answer by a `min` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/max | doc) ::link~(::map/sort | doc)
      ::link~(::desc | doc) ::link~(::vec/min | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/min/answer
  |~~ The least value, null for the empty map. ~~|
  ::any

:map/min/key
  |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/n)`, it runs on each value,
      while `(/n)` is computed once at the call, against the map, and
      refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/min/answersTheLeastValue
  |~~ The least value answers. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | min | eq 1)

:map/min/ranksANumberBeforeAString
  |~~ In the one order a number comes before a string. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b "x"} | min | eq 1)

:map/min/ranksByTheKey
  |~~ The key ranks each value. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:n 2} :b {:n 1}} | min ~(/n) | eq {:n 1})

:map/min/ranksInReverseUnderDesc
  |~~ A key under `desc` ranks in reverse. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | min ~(desc) | eq 3)

:map/min/findsTheKeyByEntries
  |~~ The key of the least value is read by `entries`. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | min ~(/1) | /0 | eq :b)

:map/min/answersNullForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map has no least value. ~~|
  ::law~({} | min | eq null)

:map/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAKeyComputedAtTheCall
  |~~ A key written without its quote is computed at the call and refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:n 1}} | min (/n)
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::map/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::proj/KeyNotInMapError])

:map/max
  |~~ Answers the greatest value in the one order of values, a string
      after a number, or null for the empty map; a key the call gives
      ranks the values, as for `min`. The entry of the greatest
      value, key and value, is `entries | max ~(/1)`. A vector and a set
      answer by a `max` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/min | doc) ::link~(::vec/max | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/max/answer
  |~~ The greatest value, null for the empty map. ~~|
  ::any

:map/max/key
  |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null, `max ~(/n)`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/max/answersTheGreatestValue
  |~~ The greatest value answers. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | max | eq 3)

:map/max/ranksAStringAfterANumber
  |~~ In the one order a string comes after a number. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b "x"} | max | eq "x")

:map/max/findsTheKeyByEntries
  |~~ The key of the greatest value is read by `entries`. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | max ~(/1) | /0 | eq :a)

:map/max/answersNullForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map has no greatest value. ~~|
  ::law~({} | max | eq null)

:map/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A key that is no quote is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | max 1 !| type | eq ::map/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError)

:map/max/ranksByTheKey
  |~~ The key the call gives ranks each value. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:age 30} :b {:age 40}} | max ~(/age) | eq {:age 40})

:map/map
  |~~ Answers the map of what the code answers for each value, under the
      same keys in their order, `{:a 1 :b 2} | map ~(add 1)` answering
      `{:a 2 :b 3}`. The code is a quote run on each value as its
      subject, not on its entry, so a key is read beside its value by
      `entries | map ~(…)`, and the pairs build a map back by `tag
      ::map`. The first error the code answers for a value is the
      answer of the call, and the answers stand in a map of their own,
      under no tag of the subject. A vector and a set answer `map` by
      words of their own.
      ::link~(::map/filter | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/map | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/map/answer
  |~~ What the code answered for each value, under the same keys, a map
      under no tag.
  ~~|
  ::any

:map/map/code
  |~~ The code run on each value, a quote. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/map/code/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The code is a quote, `map ~(add 1)`; written without the tilde,
      `map (add 1)`, it is computed once at the call, against the map,
      and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/map/keepsTheKeys
  |~~ Each value is replaced under its key. ~~|
  ::law~({:x 1 :y 2} | map ~(add 1) | eq {:x 2 :y 3})

:map/map/runsOnTheValue
  |~~ The code runs on the value, not on its entry. ~~|
  ::law~({:a [1 2]} | map ~count | eq {:a 2})

:map/map/readsAKeyThroughEntries
  |~~ A key is read beside its value through the entries. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | entries | map ~(/0) | eq [:a])

:map/map/answersTheFirstFailure
  |~~ The first error, in the order of the keys, answers the call. ~~|
  ::law~({:a "x" :b "y"} | map ~(add 1) !| /trail/0/subject | eq "x")

:map/map/answersUnderNoTag
  |~~ The answers stand under no tag of the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 1} | map ~(add 1) | eq {:a 2})

:map/map/code/NotQuoteError/refusesAStepComputedAtTheCall
  |~~ A step written without its quote is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | map (add 1) !| type | eq ::map/map/code/NotQuoteError)

:map/filter
  |~~ Answers the entries whose value the predicate answers true for,
      their keys kept, under the tags of the map: `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} |
      filter ~(gt 1)` answers `{:b 2 :c 3}`. The predicate is a quote the
      call gives, run on each value and not on its entry, so a test of a
      key reads the entries and builds the map back, `entries | filter
      ~(/0 | ne :tmp) | tag ::map`, and a key leaves by `minus`. The
      predicate answers a boolean; any other answer is refused with the
      key of its entry. A vector and a set keep their elements by a
      `filter` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/every | doc) ::link~(::map/minus | doc)
      ::link~(::map/entries | doc) ::link~(::vec/filter | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/filter/answer
  |~~ The entries whose values the predicate accepted, under the tags of
      the map.
  ~~|
  ::map

:map/filter/predicate
  |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`; written without the
      tilde, `filter (gt 1)`, it is computed once at the call and refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for the value of each
      entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError/key
  |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/filter/keepsTheKeys
  |~~ The entries kept keep their keys. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | filter ~(gt 1) | eq {:b 2 :c 3})

:map/filter/keepsNothingOfTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map filters to itself. ~~|
  ::law~({} | filter ~(gt 0) | eq {})

:map/filter/answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
  |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 1 :b 2} | filter ~(gt 1) | eq ::Box{:b 2})

:map/filter/runsOnTheValue
  |~~ The predicate meets the value, not its entry: a number has no place. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | filter ~(/0) !| type | eq ::proj/SubjectNotProjectableError)

:map/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesAPredicateComputedAtTheCall
  |~~ A predicate written without its quote is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | filter (gt 0) !| type | eq ::map/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError)

:map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | filter ~(1)
    !| [type /key /actualType]
    |  eq [::map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])

:map/every
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers true for every value, `{:a 1
      :b 2} | every ~(gt 0)` answering `true`, and `true` for the empty
      map, which holds no value to fail it. The predicate is a quote the
      call gives, run on each value, and answers a boolean; any other
      answer is refused with the key of its entry. A vector and a set ask
      their elements by an `every` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/any | doc) ::link~(::map/filter | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/every | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/every/answer
  |~~ `true` when the predicate held for every value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/every/predicate
  |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/every/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `every ~(gt 0)`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for the value of each
      entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError/key
  |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/every/holdsOfEveryValue
  |~~ Every value is positive. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0))

:map/every/asksTheValues
  |~~ A map is asked of its values, and one that fails fails `every`. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b -2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0) | not)

:map/every/holdsOfTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map holds no value to fail. ~~|
  ::law~({} | every ~(gt 0))

:map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | every ~(1)
    !| [type /key /actualType]
    |  eq [::map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])

:map/every/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesCodeComputedAtTheCall
  |~~ A predicate written without its quote is computed at the call and refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | every (gt 0) !| type | eq ::map/every/predicate/NotQuoteError)

:map/any
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers true for some value, `{:a -1
      :b 2} | any ~(gt 0)` answering `true`, and `false` for the empty
      map. The predicate is a quote the call gives, run on each value, and
      answers a boolean; any other answer is refused with the key of its
      entry. A vector and a set ask their elements by an `any` of their
      own.
      ::link~(::map/every | doc) ::link~(::map/filter | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/any | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/any/answer
  |~~ `true` when the predicate held for some value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/any/predicate
  |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/any/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `any ~(gt 0)`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for the value of each
      entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError/key
  |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/any/holdsOfSomeValue
  |~~ One positive value is enough. ~~|
  ::law~({:a -1 :b 0 :c 2} | any ~(gt 0))

:map/any/failsOfTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map holds no value to pass. ~~|
  ::law~({} | any ~(gt 0) | not)

:map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | any ~(1)
    !| [type /key /actualType]
    |  eq [::map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])

:map/any/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesCodeComputedAtTheCall
  |~~ A predicate written without its quote is computed at the call and refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | any (gt 0) !| type | eq ::map/any/predicate/NotQuoteError)

:map/sort
  |~~ Answers the entries ordered by their values in the one order, their
      keys kept, under the tags of the map: `{:a 3 :b 1} | sort` answers
      `{:b 1 :a 3}`. A key the call gives, code run on each value, ranks
      them, `sort ~(/n)`, and a key under `desc` descends,
      `sort ~(desc)`. The entries of the answer read in order by `entries`
      and `vals`, while `keys` answers its keys by name. A vector and a
      set order by a `sort` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/min | doc) ::link~(::desc | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/sort | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/sort/answer
  |~~ The entries in the order the key ranks them, under the tags of the
      map.
  ~~|
  ::map

:map/sort/key
  |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null, `sort ~(/n)`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/sort/ordersByValue
  |~~ The entries order by their values. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1 :c 2} | sort | vals | eq [1 2 3])

:map/sort/ordersByTheKey
  |~~ The key ranks each value, and each value keeps its key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:n 2} :b {:n 1}} | sort ~(/n) | entries | map ~/0 | eq [:b :a])

:map/sort/descendsUnderDesc
  |~~ A key under `desc` descends. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 3} | sort ~(desc) | vals | eq [3 1])

:map/sort/answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
  |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 3 :b 1} | sort | eq ::Box{:b 1 :a 3})

:map/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A key that is no code is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | sort 1 !| type | eq ::map/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError)

:map/take
  |~~ Answers the first entries of the map, as many as the count the call
      gives, in the order the map holds them, under the tags of the map:
      `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2` answers `{:a 1 :b 2}`. A count past the
      size takes the whole map and a count below one takes none. The
      count is a whole number. A vector, a set and a string take by a
      `take` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/drop | doc) ::link~(::map/first | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/take | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/take/answer
  |~~ The first entries, under the tags of the map. ~~|
  ::map

:map/take/count
  |~~ How many entries, a whole number. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number, `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2`; text that holds one
      is read by `parseJson` first, `take ("2" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:map/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number, `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2`; a fraction is
      rounded first, `take (1.5 | round)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:map/take/takesTheFirstEntries
  |~~ Two entries of a map are its first two. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2 | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/take/takesTheWholeMapForACountPastItsSize
  |~~ A count past the size takes every entry. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | take 99 | eq {:a 1})

:map/take/takesNothingForACountBelowOne
  |~~ A count below one takes no entry. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | take -1 | eq {})

:map/take/answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
  |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 1 :b 2} | take 1 | eq ::Box{:a 1})

:map/take/count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | take 1.5 !| type | eq ::map/take/count/NotIntegerError)

:map/take/count/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | take "1" !| type | eq ::map/take/count/NotNumberError)

:map/drop
  |~~ Answers the entries after the first ones, as many as the count the
      call gives, in the order the map holds them, under the tags of the
      map: `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2` answers `{:c 3}`. A count below one
      drops none and a count past the size drops every entry. The count
      is a whole number. A key leaves a map by `minus`, which takes the
      keys to leave out, `minus #[:b]`. A vector, a set and a string drop
      by a `drop` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/take | doc) ::link~(::map/minus | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/drop | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/drop/answer
  |~~ The entries after the ones dropped, under the tags of the map. ~~|
  ::map

:map/drop/count
  |~~ How many entries, a whole number. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number, `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2`; text that holds one
      is read by `parseJson` first, `drop ("2" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:map/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number, `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2`; a fraction is
      rounded first, `drop (1.5 | round)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:map/drop/dropsTheFirstEntries
  |~~ Dropping two leaves the last. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2 | eq {:c 3})

:map/drop/dropsNothingForACountBelowOne
  |~~ A count below one drops no entry. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | drop -1 | eq {:a 1})

:map/drop/dropsEveryEntryForACountPastTheSize
  |~~ A count past the size drops every entry. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | drop 5 | eq {})

:map/drop/count/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | drop "1" !| type | eq ::map/drop/count/NotNumberError)

:map/drop/count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A count that is no whole number is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | drop 1.5 !| type | eq ::map/drop/count/NotIntegerError)

:map/at
  |~~ Answers the value the map holds under the key the call gives, or the
      default for a key the map lacks, null when the call gives none:
      `{:a 1} | at :a` answers `1`, and `{:a 1} | at :z 0` answers `0`. A
      key is a keyword or a string, both naming the key as the map stores
      it, so `at "a b"` reads a key written in JSON as `"a b"`; a number
      names none. A key held with a null value answers null and not the
      default, which stands for a key the map lacks alone; `has` tells the
      two apart. `at` reads softly: the projection `/k` refuses a key the
      map lacks with `::proj/KeyNotInMapError`, while `at` answers null
      and chains through it, `at :a | at :b`. The key is computed against
      the subject, so `at /k` reads the key from the field `k`, and a
      field is read by the keyword that names it, `at :email`. A vector
      reads a place by an `at` of its own, and null answers the default,
      null when the call gives none.
      ::link~(::map/has | doc) ::link~(::proj | doc) ::link~(::vec/at | doc) ::link~(::null/at | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/at/answer
  |~~ The value under the key, or the default for a key the map lacks. ~~|
  ::any

:map/at/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, a keyword or a string, computed against the
      subject.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/at/key/NotGivenError
  |~~ `at` reads the value at the key its call gives, `{:a 1} | at :a`; a
      call with no key is refused.
  ~~|

:map/at/key/NotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ The key is a keyword or a string: a field is read by the keyword
      that names it, `at :email`, while `at /email` hands the value of the
      field as the key, and `:actualValue` holds what it handed.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::string)

:map/at/default
  |~~ The value answered for a key the map lacks, null when the call
      gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 1)

:map/at/readsAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword reads its value. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | at :a | eq 1)

:map/at/readsAString
  |~~ A string names the key as a keyword does. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | at "a" | eq 1)

:map/at/readsAKeyWrittenInJson
  |~~ A string reads a key written in JSON. ~~|
  ::law~({"a b": 1} | at "a b" | eq 1)

:map/at/answersNullForAMissingKey
  |~~ A key the map lacks answers null. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at :z | eq null)

:map/at/answersTheDefault
  |~~ The default answers for a key the map lacks. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at :z 0 | eq 0)

:map/at/answersNullForANullValue
  |~~ A key held with a null value answers null, not the default. ~~|
  ::law~({:a null} | at :a 0 | eq null)

:map/at/computesTheKeyAgainstTheSubject
  |~~ A field of the subject gives the key. ~~|
  ::law~({:k :a :a 1} | at /k | eq 1)

:map/at/key/NotKeywordOrStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number key is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/at/key/NotKeywordOrStringError ::number])

:map/at/key/NotKeywordOrStringError/refusesAProjectionForAKey
  |~~ A projection is computed at the call, and its answer is no key. ~~|
  ::law~({:email {:home "a@b"}} | at /email !| /actualValue | eq {:home "a@b"})

:map/at/key/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoKey
  |~~ A call with no key is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at !| type | eq ::map/at/key/NotGivenError)

:map/reverse
  |~~ Answers the entries in the reverse order, their keys kept, under the
      tags of the map: `{:a 1 :b 2} | reverse` answers `{:b 2 :a 1}`. It
      takes no value. A vector and a set reverse by a `reverse` of their
      own.
      ::link~(::map/sort | doc) ::link~(::vec/reverse | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/reverse/answer
  |~~ The entries in reverse order, under the tags of the map. ~~|
  ::map

:map/reverse/reversesTheEntries
  |~~ The entries come in reverse order. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | reverse | vals | eq [2 1])

:map/reverse/keepsTheKeys
  |~~ Each value keeps its key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | reverse | entries | first | eq [:b 2])

:map/reduce
  |~~ Answers the seed folded over the values by the reducer, in the order
      the map holds them: `{:a 1 :b 2} | reduce 0 ~(add)` answers `3`, and
      the empty map answers the seed. The reducer is a quote of one word,
      which the fold calls with the value so far as its subject and each
      value as its first arg; a quote of several steps, a literal or a
      name of no word is refused. A vector and a set fold by a `reduce` of
      their own.
      ::link~(::map/sum | doc) ::link~(::vec/reduce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/reduce/answer
  |~~ The value the fold reached, the seed for the empty map. ~~|
  ::any

:map/reduce/seed
  |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/reduce/reducer
  |~~ A quote of one word that folds a value into the value so far. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:map/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer is a quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`, after the seed: written
      first, `reduce ~(add) 0`, it stands in the seed's place and hands
      the reducer's place the seed, and written without the tilde,
      `(add)`, it is computed once at the call; either is refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError
  |~~ The quote of `reduce` names one word, which the fold calls with the
      value so far as its subject and each value as its first arg,
      `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a quote of several steps, a literal or a name of
      no word is refused.
  ~~|

:map/reduce/foldsTheValues
  |~~ The fold adds the values to the seed. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 3)

:map/reduce/answersTheSeedForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map answers the seed. ~~|
  ::law~({} | reduce 7 ~(add) | eq 7)

:map/reduce/foldsInTheOrderOfTheEntries
  |~~ The fold meets the values in the order the map holds them. ~~|
  ::law~({:a "x" :b "y"} | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "xy")

:map/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError/refusesAReducerComputedAtTheCall
  |~~ A reducer written without its quote is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | reduce 0 (add) !| type | eq ::map/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError)

:map/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError/refusesTheReducerFirst
  |~~ A reducer written before the seed hands the reducer's place the seed. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | reduce ~(add) 0
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::map/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError ::number])

:map/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError/refusesTwoSteps
  |~~ A reducer of two steps is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | reduce 0 ~(mul 2 | add) !| type | eq ::map/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError)

:map/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError/refusesALiteral
  |~~ A literal names no word. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | reduce 0 ~(1) !| type | eq ::map/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError)

:map/keys
  |~~ Answers the set of the keys, each a keyword, in the order of their
      names and not of the entries: `{:b 1 :a 2} | keys` answers `#[:a
      :b]`, while `first` and `entries` follow the entries. It takes no
      value. A key written in JSON or read by `parseJson` is a keyword
      too, `{"a b": 1} | keys` answering `#[:"a b"]`, and a map under a
      tag answers the keys beneath it. The keys come with their values by
      `entries`, and one key is asked for by `has`.
      ::link~(::map/vals | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
      ::link~(::map/has | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/keys/answer
  |~~ The keys of the map, a set in the order of their names. ~~|
  ::set

:map/keys/SubjectNotMapError
  |~~ `keys` reads a map: a vector of maps answers its keys element by
      element, `map ~keys`, and a vector's own places are its indices, read
      by `/0`.
  ~~|

:map/keys/answersASetOfKeys
  |~~ The keys are a set. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | eq #[:age :name])

:map/keys/ordersByName
  |~~ The keys sort by name, whatever the order of the entries. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | first | eq :age)

:map/keys/readsAKeyWrittenInJson
  |~~ A key written in JSON is a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~({"a b": 1} | keys | eq #[:"a b"])

:map/keys/answersTheEmptySetForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map has no keys. ~~|
  ::law~({} | keys | eq #[])

:map/keys/readsTheKeysOfEachMapByMap
  |~~ A vector of maps answers their keys by `map`. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} {:b 2}] | map ~keys | eq [#[:a] #[:b]])

:map/keys/SubjectNotMapError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number has no keys. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | keys !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/keys/SubjectNotMapError ::number])

:map/vals
  |~~ Answers the vector of the values, in the order of the entries: `{:b
      1 :a 2} | vals` answers `[1 2]`. It takes no value, and a map under a
      tag answers the values beneath it. The values with their keys are
      `entries`.
      ::link~(::map/keys | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/vals/answer
  |~~ The values of the map, in the order of the entries. ~~|
  ::vec

:map/vals/SubjectNotMapError
  |~~ `vals` reads a map: a vector is its own values, and a vector of
      maps answers their values element by element, `map ~vals`.
  ~~|

:map/vals/keepsTheOrderOfTheEntries
  |~~ The values come in the order of the entries. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | vals | eq ["Alice" 30])

:map/vals/answersTheEmptyVectorForTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map has no values. ~~|
  ::law~({} | vals | eq [])

:map/vals/SubjectNotMapError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector has no values by key. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | vals !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/vals/SubjectNotMapError ::vec])

:map/entries
  |~~ Answers the vector of the pairs `[key value]` of the map, in the
      order of the entries, each key a keyword: the map with its keys as
      data, for a step that reads a key beside its value, as `[type
      payload]` reads a tagged value. `tag ::map` builds the map back, so
      a test of a key is `entries | filter ~(/0 | ne :tmp) | tag ::map`,
      and a key is spelled with its value by turning it to text first,
      `map ~([(/0 | keyword) "@" /1] | join "")`. It takes no value, and
      the empty map answers `[]`.
      ::link~(::map/keys | doc) ::link~(::map/construct | doc)
      ::link~(::tagged/payload | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/entries/answer
  |~~ The pairs of key and value, in the order of the entries. ~~|
  ::vec

:map/entries/SubjectNotMapError
  |~~ `entries` reads a map: a vector of maps answers their pairs element
      by element, `map ~entries`.
  ~~|

:map/entries/pairsEachKeyWithItsValue
  |~~ Each entry is a pair of its key and its value. ~~|
  ::law~({:auth {:port 3000} :db {:port 5432}} | entries | first | eq [:auth {:port 3000}])

:map/entries/spellsTheKeyWithTheValue
  |~~ The key and its value spell one string. ~~|
  ::law~({:axios "^1.6.0"} | entries | map ~([(/0 | keyword) "@" /1] | join "")
    | eq ["axios@^1.6.0"])

:map/entries/filtersByKeyAndBuildsBack
  |~~ Entries filtered by key build back into a map. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :tmp 2} | entries | filter ~(/0 | ne :tmp) | tag ::map | eq {:a 1})

:map/entries/SubjectNotMapError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector has no entries. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | entries !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/entries/SubjectNotMapError ::vec])

:map/has
  |~~ Answers whether the map holds the key the call gives, a keyword or a
      string, both naming the key as the map stores it: `{:a 1} | has :a`
      answers `true`. A key held with a null value is held, `{:a null} |
      has :a` answering `true`, so `has` tells a null value from a key the
      map lacks, where `at` answers null for both. The key is computed
      against the subject, so it is written as the keyword that names it,
      `has :name`. A set asks for an element and a vector for a value by
      a `has` of their own, and a string asks for a part by `contains`.
      ::link~(::map/at | doc) ::link~(::set/has | doc)
      ::link~(::string/contains | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/has/answer
  |~~ `true` when the map holds the key, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/has/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, a keyword or a string, computed against the
      subject.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/has/key/NotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ The key is a keyword or a string, written as the keyword that names
      it, `has :name`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::string)

:map/has/findsAKeyword
  |~~ A key held is found. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice"} | has :name)

:map/has/findsAString
  |~~ A string names the key as a keyword does. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice"} | has "name")

:map/has/findsNoKeyTheMapLacks
  |~~ A key the map lacks is not held. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | has :b | not)

:map/has/holdsAKeyWithANullValue
  |~~ A key held with a null value is held. ~~|
  ::law~({:a null} | has :a)

:map/has/key/NotKeywordOrStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number key is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | has 42
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::map/has/key/NotKeywordOrStringError ::number])

:map/union
  |~~ Answers the entries of the subject together with those of the map
      the call gives, which wins a key both hold, under the tags of the
      subject: `{:a 1 :b 2} | union {:b 3 :c 4}` answers `{:a 1 :b 3 :c
      4}`. The values are not merged deeper; a key both hold takes the
      value of the other map whole. A set or a vector of keys unites with
      no map; a set and a vector unite by a `union` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/minus | doc) ::link~(::map/inter | doc)
      ::link~(::set/union | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/union/answer
  |~~ The entries of both maps, the other winning a key both hold, under
      the tags of the subject.
  ~~|
  ::map

:map/union/other
  |~~ The other map. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/union/other/NotMapError
  |~~ The subject unites with a map, `union {:b 2}`: a key comes with its
      value, and a set or a vector of keys alone is refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map)

:map/union/letsTheOtherWin
  |~~ The other map's value wins a key both hold. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | union {:b 3 :c 4} | eq {:a 1 :b 3 :c 4})

:map/union/mergesTwoMaps
  |~~ The entries of both are merged. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | union {:b 2} | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/union/takesTheOtherValueWhole
  |~~ A key both hold takes the other's value whole, not merged deeper. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:x 1}} | union {:a {:y 2}} | eq {:a {:y 2}})

:map/union/answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 1} | union {:b 2} | eq ::Box{:a 1 :b 2})

:map/union/other/NotMapError/refusesASetOfKeys
  |~~ A set of keys unites with no map. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | union #[:b] !| type | eq ::map/union/other/NotMapError)

:map/minus
  |~~ Answers the entries of the map whose keys are not among the keys the
      call gives, under the tags of the map: the keys of a map, or the
      keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword or a string, as `at`
      reads a key. `{:a 1 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp]` answers `{:a 1}`, and
      a key the map lacks changes nothing. A single key is written in a
      set, `minus #[:tmp]`. A set and a vector take out their elements by
      a `minus` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/inter | doc) ::link~(::map/union | doc)
      ::link~(::set/minus | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/minus/answer
  |~~ The entries whose keys are not among the keys, under the tags of
      the map.
  ~~|
  ::map

:map/minus/keys
  |~~ The keys to leave out: a map, or a vector or a set of keys. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/minus/keys/NotMapOrVecError
  |~~ The keys are a map, or a vector or a set of keys; a single key is
      written in a set, `minus #[:tmp]`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map ::vec)

:map/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the other value that is neither.
  ~~|

:map/minus/leavesTheKeysOfASet
  |~~ The keys a set names leave. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/minus/leavesTheKeysOfAVector
  |~~ A string in a vector names a key to leave. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | minus ["a"] | eq {:b 2})

:map/minus/leavesTheKeysOfAMap
  |~~ The keys of a map leave, whatever their values. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | minus {:b 9} | eq {:a 1})

:map/minus/keepsTheMapForAKeyItLacks
  |~~ A key the map lacks changes nothing. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | minus [:z] | eq {:a 1})

:map/minus/answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
  |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 1 :b 2} | minus [:a] | eq ::Box{:b 2})

:map/minus/keys/NotMapOrVecError/refusesASingleKey
  |~~ A key written alone is refused; a set holds it. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | minus "a" !| type | eq ::map/minus/keys/NotMapOrVecError)

:map/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | minus [:a 1]
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::map/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::number 1])

:map/inter
  |~~ Answers the entries of the map whose keys are among the keys the
      call gives, under the tags of the map: the keys of a map, or the
      keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword or a string. `{:a 1 :b
      2 :c 3} | inter #[:a :b]` answers `{:a 1 :b 2}`, and keys the map
      lacks keep none of its entries. A set and a vector keep the
      elements they share by an `inter` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/minus | doc) ::link~(::set/inter | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/inter/answer
  |~~ The entries whose keys are among the keys, under the tags of the map. ~~|
  ::map

:map/inter/keys
  |~~ The keys to keep: a map, or a vector or a set of keys. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/inter/keys/NotMapOrVecError
  |~~ The keys are a map, or a vector or a set of keys, `inter #[:a]`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map ::vec)

:map/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the other value that is neither.
  ~~|

:map/inter/keepsTheKeysOfASet
  |~~ The keys a set names stay. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter #[:a :b] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/inter/keepsTheKeysOfAVector
  |~~ Strings and keywords in a vector name the keys to keep. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter ["a" :c] | eq {:a 1 :c 3})

:map/inter/keepsTheKeysOfAMap
  |~~ The keys of a map stay, whatever their values. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | inter {:b 9} | eq {:b 2})

:map/inter/keepsNoEntryForKeysTheMapLacks
  |~~ Keys the map lacks keep none of its entries. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | inter [:z] | eq {})

:map/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | inter [[:a]]
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::map/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::vec 0])

:map/inter/keys/NotMapOrVecError/refusesANumber
  |~~ Keys that are no map, vector or set are refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | inter 5 !| type | eq ::map/inter/keys/NotMapOrVecError)

:map/raise
  |~~ Answers the map as an error on the fail track, the act the literal
      `!{…}` writes with its bang, so a query fails where its own check
      fails. Its tag is the one its value shows: the outermost tag over
      the map, then the one the map carries, `::Oops{:message "boom"} |
      raise`, then `::error`, every entry of the map a fact; and an
      opened error raised again is itself. `|` steps around the
      raised error into its trail, and `!|` opens it to its facts. It
      takes no value: what the error says is a fact of the map. A value
      of another kind is put in a map first, `{:value /} | raise`.
      ::link~(::error | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/raise/answer
  |~~ The error the map is raised as, on the fail track. ~~|
  ::error

:map/raise/SubjectNotMapError
  |~~ `raise` raises a map: a value of another kind is put in one first,
      `{:value /} | raise`, or the error is written as a literal,
      `!{:reason :late}`.
  ~~|

:map/raise/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `raise` takes no value: what the error says is a fact of the map,
      `{:message "boom"} | raise`.
  ~~|

:map/raise/raisesTheSubject
  |~~ `{…} | raise` raises the subject, a map the step builds among them. ~~|
  ::law~({:message "boom"} | raise !| /message | eq "boom")

:map/raise/takesTheTagTheMapCarries
  |~~ The tag the map carries is the tag of the error. ~~|
  ::law~(::Oops{:message "boom"} | raise !| type | eq ::Oops)

:map/raise/keepsEveryEntryAsAFact
  |~~ Every entry of the map is a fact of the error, a `:kind` among them. ~~|
  ::law~({:kind ::Oops} | raise !| [type /kind] | eq [::error ::Oops])

:map/raise/raisesAnOpenedErrorAsItself
  |~~ An opened error raised again is itself. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| raise !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)

:map/raise/renamesByATag
  |~~ A step that lays a tag over an opened error renames it. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| tag ::Renamed | raise !| type | eq ::Renamed)

:map/raise/guardsAValue
  |~~ A guard raises an error of its own tag when a value fails its check. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | if (gt 100) ~(/) ~(::TooSmall{:value /} | raise)
    !| [type /value]
    |  eq [::TooSmall 42])

:map/raise/SubjectNotMapError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no map to raise. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | raise !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/raise/SubjectNotMapError ::number])

:map/raise/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAMessage
  |~~ A message given to `raise` is refused; the map holds it. ~~|
  ::law~({:kind :oops} | raise "boom" !| type | eq ::map/raise/ArgsBeyondPartsError)
